-- title:
--   Independence and uniform families of matroids, density, and the loop/coloop/uniform component condition
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathsf M$ be a matroid on the ground set $E=\{1,\dots,n\}$ of users, listed in arrival order.
--
--   1. Its **independence family** $\mathcal I(\mathsf M)$ is the family of independent sets; these are the feasible sets when the constraint is $\mathsf M$. Its minimally infeasible sets are exactly the circuits, so the components of the family (classes of the relation generated by "lying in a common minimally infeasible set") are the connected components of $\mathsf M$.
--   2. The **uniform matroid** $U_{k,n}$ has as feasible sets all $S\subseteq E$ with $|S|\le k$. Single selection is $U_{1,n}$, and $M_k=\mathbb E[\text{sum of the }k\text{ largest values}]$ is the prophet value of $U_{k,n}$.
--   3. The **density** of $\mathsf M$ after its loops are removed is
--   $$d(\mathsf M)=\max\Big\{\frac{|S|}{\operatorname{rk}(S)} : \emptyset\ne S\subseteq E,\ S \text{ contains no loop}\Big\}.$$
--   For a loopless matroid this is $d=\max_{S\ne\emptyset}|S|/\operatorname{rk}(S)$.
--   4. A set $C$ carries the **uniform restriction of rank $k$** if a subset of $C$ is feasible exactly when it has at most $k$ elements. The component condition of Theorem 2 says that every connected component $C$, with $m=|C|$, is a loop ($U_{0,1}$), a coloop ($U_{1,1}$), $U_{1,m}$ or $U_{m-1,m}$.
--
--   These objects state Theorems 2, 4, 6, 7 and 18 of the paper on top of the published family layer of the mission.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The matroid is Mathlib's `Matroid (Fin n)`; the theorems assume its ground set is all of `Fin n`. The rank is Mathlib's `eRk`, finite on `Fin n` and read as a natural number; it is at least $1$ on a nonempty set of non-loops. If every element is a loop, the maximum in the density ranges over nothing and the Lean value is $0$; no statement uses it in that case. Components are the published `LDPSelect.comp`.
-- source:
--   Chen, Wang, When Does Private Data Collection Need Interaction? Online Selection under Local Differential Privacy, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 2e2d6157…), §1.1, pp.2–3 (pdf pp.3–4), Theorems 2, 4, 7; §3, p.6 (pdf p.7); §8, p.11 (pdf p.12); §9, p.13 (pdf p.14)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LDPSelect_Family
import Definitions.Def_LDPSelect_InClass

namespace LDPSelect.Supplement

/-!
# Matroid families: independence family, uniform family, density, component types

Chen, Wang, *When Does Private Data Collection Need Interaction? Online Selection under Local
Differential Privacy*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 2e2d6157…), §1.1, pp.2–3 (Theorems 2,
4, 7); §3, p.6; §8, p.11; §9, p.13.

**Formalization Note.**
- A matroid is Mathlib's `Matroid (Fin n)`; the theorems that use it assume `M.E = Set.univ`, so the
  ground set is the whole arrival sequence `Fin n`. Its feasible sets are `indepFam M`.
- The minimally infeasible sets (`LDPSelect.IsMIS`) of `indepFam M` are the circuits, so the published
  `LDPSelect.comp` gives the connected components of the matroid.
- `density M` is the paper's density of `M` *after its loops are removed*: the maximum of
  `|S| / rk(S)` over nonempty sets `S` of non-loops. For a loopless matroid it is the paper's
  `d = max_{S ≠ ∅} |S|/rk(S)`. The rank is Mathlib's `eRk`, finite on `Fin n`, read as a natural
  number; it is at least `1` on a nonempty set of non-loops. If every element is a loop the index type
  is empty and the value is `0`; no theorem uses it in that case.
- `IsUnifOn F C k` says that the restriction of `F` to `C` is the uniform matroid of rank `k` on `C`.
-/

open LDPSelect

/-- The independence family of a matroid on `Fin n`: the sets `S` with `M.Indep S` (§3, p.6). -/
def indepFam {n : ℕ} (M : Matroid (Fin n)) : Family n :=
  {S | M.Indep (↑S : Set (Fin n))}

/-- The uniform matroid `U_{k,n}`: the sets of size at most `k` (§1.1, p.2; §8, p.11). -/
def unifFam (k n : ℕ) : Family n :=
  {S | S.card ≤ k}

/-- The density of `M` after its loops are removed: `max |S| / rk(S)` over nonempty sets `S` of
non-loops (§1.1, p.2; §9, p.13). -/
noncomputable def density {n : ℕ} (M : Matroid (Fin n)) : ℝ :=
  ⨆ S : {S : Finset (Fin n) // S.Nonempty ∧ ∀ e ∈ S, ¬ M.IsLoop e},
    (S.1.card : ℝ) / ((M.eRk (↑S.1 : Set (Fin n))).toNat : ℝ)

/-- The restriction of `F` to `C` is the uniform matroid of rank `k` on `C`: a subset of `C` is
feasible exactly when it has at most `k` elements. -/
def IsUnifOn {n : ℕ} (F : Family n) (C : Set (Fin n)) (k : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ S : Finset (Fin n), (↑S : Set (Fin n)) ⊆ C → (S ∈ F ↔ S.card ≤ k)

/-- Every connected component `C` (with `m = |C|`) is a loop (`U_{0,1}`), a coloop (`U_{1,1}`),
`U_{1,m}` or `U_{m−1,m}` (Theorem 2, p.2). -/
def IsLoopColoopUnifComps {n : ℕ} (F : Family n) : Prop :=
  ∀ u : Fin n,
    ((comp F u).ncard = 1 ∧ IsUnifOn F (comp F u) 0) ∨
    ((comp F u).ncard = 1 ∧ IsUnifOn F (comp F u) 1) ∨
    IsUnifOn F (comp F u) 1 ∨
    IsUnifOn F (comp F u) ((comp F u).ncard - 1)

end LDPSelect.Supplement


